HashMap Data Structure - Full Beginner Guide
(English + Hinglish Explanation)

What is a HashMap?
A HashMap is a key-value pair data structure in Java that allows fast retrieval, insertion, and deletion of elements.
(Ye ek key-value pair data structure hai jo fast retrieval, insertion aur deletion provide karta hai.)

It is part of Java’s java.util package.
(Ye Java ke java.util package me hota hai.)

Why Use HashMap?
Fast Lookup – O(1) average time complexity.
(Search karna fast hota hai, kyunki average case me O(1) time lagta hai.)
No Duplicate Keys – Only unique keys are allowed.
(Sirf unique keys allowed hoti hain, duplicate nahi hoti.)
Efficient Data Storage – Helps store and retrieve data quickly.
(Efficient storage ke liye use hota hai, data jaldi retrieve hota hai.)
Key-Value Mapping – Useful when you need to map one value to another.
(Jab ek value ko kisi dusri value se map karna ho to helpful hota hai.)
How HashMap Works Internally?
HashMap stores data in an array of nodes (buckets).
(HashMap ek array of nodes me data store karta hai.)
It uses a hash function to calculate the index where a key-value pair should be stored.
(Ek hash function use karta hai jo key ka index find karta hai.)
If two keys produce the same index (collision), then HashMap uses LinkedList or Tree to resolve it.
(Agar collision hota hai, to HashMap LinkedList ya Tree ka use karta hai data ko store karne ke liye.)
Basic Operations in HashMap
1. Creating a HashMap
java
Copy
Edit
import java.util.HashMap;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Creating a HashMap
        HashMap<Integer, String> map = new HashMap<>();

        // Adding elements (put method)
        map.put(1, "Apple");
        map.put(2, "Banana");
        map.put(3, "Orange");

        System.out.println(map);
    }
}
Output:

plaintext
Copy
Edit
{1=Apple, 2=Banana, 3=Orange}
Explanation:

put(key, value) method se data add kiya jata hai.
{1=Apple, 2=Banana, 3=Orange} ka matlab hai ki key 1 ka value "Apple" hai, key 2 ka "Banana" hai, etc.
2. Accessing Elements
java
Copy
Edit
System.out.println(map.get(1)); // Output: Apple
Explanation:

get(key) method se value retrieve hoti hai.
3. Checking if Key Exists
java
Copy
Edit
System.out.println(map.containsKey(2)); // Output: true
Explanation:

containsKey(key) check karta hai ki key available hai ya nahi.
4. Checking if Value Exists
java
Copy
Edit
System.out.println(map.containsValue("Grapes")); // Output: false
Explanation:

containsValue(value) check karta hai ki koi value exist karti hai ya nahi.
5. Removing an Element
java
Copy
Edit
map.remove(2);
System.out.println(map); // Output: {1=Apple, 3=Orange}
Explanation:

remove(key) se key-value pair delete hota hai.
6. Iterating Over a HashMap
java
Copy
Edit
for (Integer key : map.keySet()) {
    System.out.println("Key: " + key + ", Value: " + map.get(key));
}
Explanation:

keySet() se saari keys milti hain, aur get(key) se value retrieve hoti hai.
Time Complexity of HashMap Operations
Operation	Average Case	Worst Case (Collision)
Insertion (put)	O(1)	O(n)
Deletion (remove)	O(1)	O(n)
Searching (get)	O(1)	O(n)
When to Use HashMap?
✅ Jab aapko key-value mapping chahiye.
✅ Jab aapko fast lookup/search chahiye.
✅ Jab aapko unique keys store karni ho.

When NOT to Use HashMap?
❌ Jab aapko ordering maintain karni ho, use LinkedHashMap instead.
❌ Jab aapko sorted keys chahiye, use TreeMap.

Conclusion
HashMap ek powerful data structure hai jo fast retrieval provide karta hai.
Key-value pairs me data store karta hai.
Collision handling ke liye LinkedList ya Tree ka use karta hai.
Fast operations (O(1)) provide karta hai most cases me.
Ab aapko HashMap ka basic understanding mil gaya! 🚀
